## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128-bit data as four independent 32-bit lanes and perform lane-wise rotation without loops. Demonstrates precise bit-slicing, correct boundary handling, and minimal, provable code paths.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit packing into 4×32 lanes
- Lane-wise rotation using (x<<n) | (x>>(32-n))
- Bit masking to confine intermediate results to 32-bit lanes
- Clamping n with & 31 to handle n >= 32

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64 lo; uint64 hi; } uint128;

uint128 rotateWithinLanes128(uint128 x, unsigned n) {
    n &= 31;
    uint32 a = (uint32)(x.lo);
    uint32 b = (uint32)(x.lo >> 32);
    uint32 c = (uint32)(x.hi);
    uint32 d = (uint32)(x.hi >> 32);

    x.lo = ((uint64)((a << n) | (a >> (32 - n))) << 32) |
           ((b << n) | (b >> (32 - n)));
    x.hi = ((uint64)((c << n) | (c >> (32 - n))) << 32) |
           ((d << n) | (d >> (32 - n)));
    return x;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement a constant-time popcount-style operation using only masks and shifts, simulating a common bitmap diff in games or databases.

## Key Concepts
- Hamming weight via parallel reduction
- XOR to identify differences
- Mask sequences for bit counting

## Code Example
```cpp
int hammingDistance64(uint64 a, uint64 b){
  uint64 x = a ^ b;
  x = (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) + ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL) + ((x >> 4) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL);
  x = (x & 0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL) + ((x >> 8) & 0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL);
  x = (x & 0x0000ffff0000ffffULL) + ((x >> 16) & 0x0000ffff0000ffffULL);
  x = (x & 0x00000000ffffffffULL) + ((x >> 32) & 0x00000000ffffffffULL);
  return (int)x;
}
```

